meta {
  name: upload-image-url
  type: http
  seq: 7
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/admin/v1/menu/items/{{admin_item_uid}}/image/upload
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{access_token}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "content_type": "image/jpeg",
    "size_bytes": 184320
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.status === 200 && res.body.data) {
    bru.setVar("image_object_key", res.body.data.object_key)
  }
}

tests {
  test("responds 200, or 501 where this deployment stores no images", function () {
    expect([200, 501]).to.include(res.status)
  })

  test("the object key names this restaurant and this dish", function () {
    if (res.status === 200) {
      const key = res.body.data.object_key
      expect(key).to.match(/^menu\/rst_[0-9a-z]{12}\/itm_[0-9a-z]{12}\/img_[0-9a-z]{12}\.jpg$/)
      expect(key).to.include(bru.getVar("admin_item_uid"))
    }
  })

  test("headers omit the two a browser refuses to set", function () {
    if (res.status === 200) {
      const names = Object.keys(res.body.data.headers).map((h) => h.toLowerCase())
      expect(names).to.not.include("host")
      expect(names).to.not.include("content-length")
    }
  })

  test("a deployment without storage says so distinctly", function () {
    if (res.status === 501) {
      expect(res.body.code).to.equal("TX_IMG_001")
    }
  })
}

docs {
  # Get an upload URL for a dish photo

**Step one of two.** This mints a presigned URL; it does not change the dish. The browser then
PUTs the file straight to Cloudflare R2 and calls `confirm-image` -- so a 5MB photograph never
passes through this API, which runs on a 512MB instance where streaming one would occupy a
request worker for the length of a restaurant's uplink.

`content_type` must be `image/jpeg`, `image/png` or `image/webp`. **SVG is refused
deliberately** (`TX_IMG_002`): an SVG executes script in whichever browser renders it, and these
objects are served from a host of ours.

Both fields are signed into the URL, so R2 rejects a body of a different length or type. They are
still only a *claim* at this point, which is why `confirm-image` re-checks the object that
actually arrived rather than trusting them.

`object_key` encodes the restaurant and the menu item -- `menu/{restaurant_uid}/{item_uid}/{image_uid}.jpg`.
That is the mapping the whole feature turns on, and `confirm-image` refuses any key naming a
different dish or a different restaurant.

The `headers` map must be replayed on the PUT **verbatim**; they are inside the signature. `Host`
and `Content-Length` are absent on purpose -- browsers forbid script from setting either and
supply both themselves.

Answers **501 `TX_IMG_001`** on a deployment with no `TABLEX_R2_*` configuration. Not 500 and not
503: nothing has failed and nothing is down, this deployment simply does not host images. The
admin panel reads `image_upload_enabled` from `get-menu` and hides the control, so a manager
should never see it.
}
